qs.py   Strongest overall submission: it cleanly builds and demonstrates the `Book` object from argv input, includes genuinely helpful comments, and uses specific error handling that protects common failure paths. Did NOT catch exception when I gave it an ASCII endoded file with ASCII characters in it.  
ez.py   Nice, readable work with clear method intent and enough comments; with a valid UTF-8 pathname argument it executes and demonstrates the class well, though file-open exceptions are still uncovered.
37.py   ONLY one in this batch that actually, gracefully caught that I passed it a ANSII encoded file, with ANSII specific chars in it.  You covered the assignment thoroughly with several useful methods and visible commentary, and the program runs on valid UTF-8 input; the main weakness is one broad catch-all exception branch.
rh.py   This one does satisfy the prompt and executes on valid UTF-8 argv input, with three `Book` methods and basic comments; handling for missing argument and bad paths is present and functional.
97.py   Good structure and documentation make this easy to follow, and it executes correctly on valid UTF-8 argv input, but file-access failures still fall through to an uncaught traceback.
n4.py   Core behavior is valid and it runs with a proper UTF-8 file argument, but the submission is very barebones, with almost no comments and no defensive handling for bad argv or file errors.
dg.py   Method implementation and commenting are decent, but the demo uses a hardcoded path instead of command-line pathname input, so it misses a key requirement even though the script itself can run